1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,734
He went down hard on his bike,

2
00:00:01,754 --> 00:00:04,000
and there's a bad cut on his left forearm,

3
00:00:04,025 --> 00:00:05,207
on the thumb side.

4
00:00:05,377 --> 00:00:07,849
The paramedic radios ahead that it's just

5
00:00:07,899 --> 00:00:08,754
above the wrist.

6
00:00:08,830 --> 00:00:10,716
But watch what happens when he lifts his

7
00:00:10,811 --> 00:00:11,611
arm.

8
00:00:11,641 --> 00:00:14,339
Now above the wrist points the other way,

9
00:00:14,383 --> 00:00:15,698
toward his fingers.

10
00:00:16,282 --> 00:00:17,666
So which one did she mean?

11
00:00:18,017 --> 00:00:20,533
By the time we're done, you'll be able to

12
00:00:20,583 --> 00:00:22,949
describe any spot on the body so it means

13
00:00:23,024 --> 00:00:25,854
the SAME thing to everyone who hears it.

14
00:00:25,939 --> 00:00:28,609
And words like hep a toe meg a lee will

15
00:00:28,684 --> 00:00:30,647
stop looking like a foreign language,

16
00:00:30,694 --> 00:00:32,835
because you'll take them apart into pieces

17
00:00:32,873 --> 00:00:34,194
you already know.

18
00:00:35,149 --> 00:00:37,590
If this part of the course feels like memorizing

19
00:00:37,760 --> 00:00:39,250
a dictionary, that's normal.

20
00:00:39,401 --> 00:00:41,892
But medical words are built from a small

21
00:00:41,967 --> 00:00:44,383
set of parts that keep their meaning wherever

22
00:00:44,413 --> 00:00:45,213
they show up.

23
00:00:45,383 --> 00:00:47,930
And directions only feel slippery because

24
00:00:48,005 --> 00:00:51,477
words like above change when the body moves,

25
00:00:51,552 --> 00:00:54,420
so anatomy describes every body from one

26
00:00:54,479 --> 00:00:57,364
standard pose that everybody agrees on.

27
00:01:00,864 --> 00:01:02,449
Twenty minutes later he's sitting in the

28
00:01:02,501 --> 00:01:04,486
emergency department, and the nurse pulls

29
00:01:04,536 --> 00:01:05,336
up his chart.

30
00:01:05,430 --> 00:01:07,958
The first thing she sees is a note from

31
00:01:07,983 --> 00:01:08,939
an old visit.

32
00:01:09,109 --> 00:01:12,637
History of coal ee sis tie tis.

33
00:01:12,679 --> 00:01:14,071
Reported gas tral ja.

34
00:01:14,222 --> 00:01:16,562
Exam showed hep a toe meg a lee.

35
00:01:17,744 --> 00:01:20,033
It looks like a different language,

36
00:01:20,071 --> 00:01:22,524
but it's really three short sentences built

37
00:01:22,554 --> 00:01:24,901
from a handful of word parts.

38
00:01:25,015 --> 00:01:27,203
Most terms are built around a ROOT.

39
00:01:27,294 --> 00:01:29,392
That's the part with the main meaning,

40
00:01:29,430 --> 00:01:31,656
and in this course it's usually a body part.

41
00:01:31,908 --> 00:01:34,901
In cardiology the root is kar dee,

42
00:01:34,939 --> 00:01:38,298
which means heart, and luh jee on the end

43
00:01:38,361 --> 00:01:39,656
means the study of.

44
00:01:39,983 --> 00:01:43,203
In gastritis the root is gas ter,

45
00:01:43,266 --> 00:01:44,637
which means stomach.

46
00:01:44,845 --> 00:01:47,732
And a word can have more than one root,

47
00:01:47,807 --> 00:01:51,656
so gastro enter ology has gas ter for stomach

48
00:01:51,750 --> 00:01:54,750
and enter for intestine, and it's the study

49
00:01:54,851 --> 00:01:55,651
of both.

50
00:01:55,864 --> 00:01:59,052
Now try saying cardilogy out loud.

51
00:01:59,203 --> 00:02:00,486
It's clunky, right?

52
00:02:00,788 --> 00:02:03,732
So medical words slip in a linking vowel,

53
00:02:03,779 --> 00:02:06,449
usually an O, and you get cardiology.

54
00:02:06,977 --> 00:02:10,147
A root plus that vowel is called a combining

55
00:02:10,222 --> 00:02:12,335
form, and it's written with a slash,

56
00:02:12,396 --> 00:02:14,826
like kar dee slash o.

57
00:02:15,090 --> 00:02:17,996
The vowel stays between two roots,

58
00:02:18,041 --> 00:02:21,543
like in gastro entero luh jee,

59
00:02:21,769 --> 00:02:24,859
and it stays before an ending that starts

60
00:02:24,879 --> 00:02:28,939
with a consonant, like meg uh lee in hep

61
00:02:29,090 --> 00:02:31,203
at o meg uh lee.

62
00:02:31,486 --> 00:02:34,222
But it drops before an ending that starts

63
00:02:34,252 --> 00:02:36,713
with a vowel, which is why it's gastritis

64
00:02:36,788 --> 00:02:38,977
and not gastro eye tis.

65
00:02:39,188 --> 00:02:41,015
Your turn.

66
00:02:41,216 --> 00:02:45,392
Neur means nerve, and luh jee means the

67
00:02:45,455 --> 00:02:46,255
study of.

68
00:02:46,316 --> 00:02:48,562
Put them together, and then tell me why

69
00:02:48,587 --> 00:02:50,411
there's an O in the middle.

70
00:02:55,999 --> 00:02:57,849
It's neurology.

71
00:02:57,932 --> 00:03:00,075
That O is the linking vowel,

72
00:03:00,113 --> 00:03:03,018
and it stays because luh jee starts with

73
00:03:03,056 --> 00:03:03,856
a consonant.

74
00:03:07,349 --> 00:03:10,366
A PREFIX goes in front and changes what

75
00:03:10,386 --> 00:03:12,179
the rest of the word means,

76
00:03:12,216 --> 00:03:13,990
and it often tells you where,

77
00:03:14,028 --> 00:03:15,952
how much, or how fast.

78
00:03:16,118 --> 00:03:20,028
Take nee uh, the word part for breathing.

79
00:03:20,292 --> 00:03:23,952
Tack ip nee uh is fast breathing,

80
00:03:24,028 --> 00:03:27,122
brad ip nee uh is slow breathing,

81
00:03:27,179 --> 00:03:30,066
and ap nee uh is no breathing at all.

82
00:03:30,198 --> 00:03:32,405
The breathing part never changed,

83
00:03:32,466 --> 00:03:33,688
only the front did.

84
00:03:34,455 --> 00:03:36,632
Watch out for the prefixes that look almost

85
00:03:36,669 --> 00:03:37,839
the same.

86
00:03:37,952 --> 00:03:42,442
Inter means between, so intercostal is between

87
00:03:42,462 --> 00:03:45,839
the ribs, but intra means inside,

88
00:03:45,940 --> 00:03:49,009
so intravenous is inside a vein.

89
00:03:49,179 --> 00:03:52,330
And hyper means more than normal,

90
00:03:52,367 --> 00:03:54,669
while hypo means less than normal.

91
00:03:54,790 --> 00:03:57,083
They're just a letter or two apart,

92
00:03:57,103 --> 00:03:59,122
but they mean very different things.

93
00:03:59,330 --> 00:04:01,989
The SUFFIX sits at the end,

94
00:04:02,009 --> 00:04:04,028
and it tells you what kind of word you're

95
00:04:04,066 --> 00:04:04,702
reading.

96
00:04:04,722 --> 00:04:07,122
Some endings name a condition,

97
00:04:07,167 --> 00:04:09,688
like eye tis for inflamed,

98
00:04:09,889 --> 00:04:15,047
al juh for pain, and meg uh lee for enlarged.

99
00:04:15,179 --> 00:04:17,537
And some just mean having to do with,

100
00:04:17,583 --> 00:04:19,500
like the ac in cardiac.

101
00:04:19,638 --> 00:04:22,292
There are two surgical endings that trip

102
00:04:22,357 --> 00:04:25,084
almost everyone up, so let's try them both

103
00:04:25,134 --> 00:04:28,028
on the kidney, which is nef er.

104
00:04:28,481 --> 00:04:32,858
Tomy means cutting into, so in a neh frot

105
00:04:32,933 --> 00:04:36,028
uh mee the surgeon cuts into the kidney,

106
00:04:36,066 --> 00:04:37,613
and it's still there afterward.

107
00:04:38,028 --> 00:04:40,632
Ectomy means cutting out,

108
00:04:40,707 --> 00:04:44,103
so in a neh frek tuh mee the kidney comes

109
00:04:44,179 --> 00:04:45,990
out, and it's gone.

110
00:04:46,111 --> 00:04:48,707
Back with our cyclist, the nurse notices

111
00:04:48,820 --> 00:04:51,423
a swollen, dark bruise on his elbow from

112
00:04:51,443 --> 00:04:55,575
the fall, and on his chart she writes subcutaneous

113
00:04:55,663 --> 00:04:56,463
hematoma.

114
00:04:56,682 --> 00:04:58,518
So what did she just write?

115
00:04:58,699 --> 00:05:00,179
Here's what you need.

116
00:05:00,443 --> 00:05:05,009
Sub means under, cue tane means skin,

117
00:05:05,134 --> 00:05:09,160
hee mat means blood, and oh muh means a

118
00:05:09,266 --> 00:05:10,669
mass or a swelling.

119
00:05:10,877 --> 00:05:13,537
Put it together and tell me where it is

120
00:05:13,632 --> 00:05:14,896
and what it is.

121
00:05:21,497 --> 00:05:23,333
It's a collection of blood,

122
00:05:23,409 --> 00:05:25,975
a hematoma, sitting under the skin,

123
00:05:26,050 --> 00:05:28,843
which is what subcutaneous means,

124
00:05:28,918 --> 00:05:31,183
so it's basically a bad bruise.

125
00:05:34,683 --> 00:05:36,456
So how do you actually read one?

126
00:05:36,560 --> 00:05:38,305
You start with the suffix,

127
00:05:38,350 --> 00:05:41,022
then the prefix, and then the root.

128
00:05:41,067 --> 00:05:43,362
Take pair ee kar die tis.

129
00:05:43,588 --> 00:05:47,437
Eye tis means inflamed, so it's a condition.

130
00:05:47,633 --> 00:05:51,890
Pair ee means around, and kard is just kar

131
00:05:51,947 --> 00:05:53,400
dee again, heart.

132
00:05:53,676 --> 00:05:56,569
So it's inflammation around the heart,

133
00:05:56,607 --> 00:05:59,059
and in practice that means the pericardium,

134
00:05:59,079 --> 00:06:00,418
the sac that wraps it.

135
00:06:00,554 --> 00:06:03,211
Read it left to right and you'd get around

136
00:06:03,274 --> 00:06:06,494
heart inflamed, which is backwards English.

137
00:06:06,635 --> 00:06:09,098
Let's go back to that old note on his chart.

138
00:06:09,362 --> 00:06:13,173
Koh lee sis tie tis ends in eye tis,

139
00:06:13,286 --> 00:06:16,192
and koh lee means gall, or bile,

140
00:06:16,324 --> 00:06:19,739
and sist means bladder, so it's an inflamed

141
00:06:19,800 --> 00:06:20,600
gallbladder.

142
00:06:20,890 --> 00:06:24,947
Gas tral juh is al juh on gas ter,

143
00:06:25,123 --> 00:06:26,909
so that's stomach pain.

144
00:06:27,022 --> 00:06:30,909
And hep a toe meg a lee is meg uh lee on

145
00:06:30,984 --> 00:06:33,249
hep at, which means liver,

146
00:06:33,324 --> 00:06:34,833
so the liver was enlarged.

147
00:06:35,975 --> 00:06:36,549
See?

148
00:06:36,569 --> 00:06:38,230
None of that is gibberish anymore.

149
00:06:39,331 --> 00:06:40,059
Quick check.

150
00:06:40,079 --> 00:06:43,362
A patient had a neh frek tuh mee last year.

151
00:06:43,543 --> 00:06:45,852
Does that patient still have that whole

152
00:06:45,906 --> 00:06:46,706
kidney?

153
00:06:52,309 --> 00:06:53,328
No.

154
00:06:53,460 --> 00:06:55,403
Ectomy means cutting out,

155
00:06:55,453 --> 00:06:57,064
so that kidney was removed.

156
00:06:57,290 --> 00:06:59,781
If it had been a neh frot uh mee,

157
00:06:59,856 --> 00:07:01,516
the kidney would still be there,

158
00:07:01,547 --> 00:07:02,800
with a cut that healed.

159
00:07:06,300 --> 00:07:07,507
Now the directions.

160
00:07:07,549 --> 00:07:09,545
Remember the radio call, just above the

161
00:07:09,595 --> 00:07:10,110
wrist?

162
00:07:10,130 --> 00:07:12,413
The trouble is, everyday direction words

163
00:07:12,466 --> 00:07:15,432
depend on how the body happens to be posed,

164
00:07:15,482 --> 00:07:17,771
so anatomy describes everything from one

165
00:07:17,821 --> 00:07:20,639
standard pose, and it's called the anatomical

166
00:07:20,723 --> 00:07:21,922
POSITION.

167
00:07:21,988 --> 00:07:23,809
Picture someone standing up straight and

168
00:07:23,874 --> 00:07:26,300
facing forward, with their head level and

169
00:07:26,325 --> 00:07:27,809
their eyes looking straight ahead.

170
00:07:27,948 --> 00:07:31,054
Their feet are flat on the floor and parallel,

171
00:07:31,084 --> 00:07:32,941
with the toes pointing forward.

172
00:07:33,016 --> 00:07:35,130
And their arms hang down at their sides,

173
00:07:35,160 --> 00:07:37,167
with the palms turned forward,

174
00:07:37,217 --> 00:07:39,356
so the thumbs point away from the body.

175
00:07:39,533 --> 00:07:42,375
Every directional term describes the body

176
00:07:42,425 --> 00:07:44,262
as if it were standing like this,

177
00:07:44,312 --> 00:07:45,998
no matter how it's really posed.

178
00:07:46,300 --> 00:07:49,620
So a patient lying face up is supine,

179
00:07:49,646 --> 00:07:52,488
which you can remember as on your spine,

180
00:07:52,526 --> 00:07:55,130
and lying face down is prone,

181
00:07:55,190 --> 00:07:57,243
like lying with your nose to the floor.

182
00:07:57,450 --> 00:07:59,733
But those words describe how the body is

183
00:07:59,809 --> 00:08:02,450
lying, and they don't change what any direction

184
00:08:02,514 --> 00:08:03,185
means.

185
00:08:03,205 --> 00:08:06,149
One more rule, and it catches a lot of people.

186
00:08:06,312 --> 00:08:09,243
Right and left always mean the patient's

187
00:08:09,337 --> 00:08:10,601
own right and left.

188
00:08:10,828 --> 00:08:12,488
So when you face someone,

189
00:08:12,538 --> 00:08:15,281
their right hand is on YOUR left.

190
00:08:15,492 --> 00:08:18,375
That's why, when a diagram shows a person

191
00:08:18,405 --> 00:08:21,243
from the front, their right side is on the

192
00:08:21,333 --> 00:08:22,526
left of the page.

193
00:08:22,737 --> 00:08:24,035
Your turn.

194
00:08:24,318 --> 00:08:27,733
The doctor walks in and stands facing our

195
00:08:27,809 --> 00:08:28,581
cyclist.

196
00:08:28,601 --> 00:08:31,507
His cut is on his left forearm,

197
00:08:31,608 --> 00:08:33,922
so is it on the doctor's left side,

198
00:08:34,073 --> 00:08:36,375
or the doctor's right?

199
00:08:41,989 --> 00:08:43,926
It's on the doctor's right.

200
00:08:44,198 --> 00:08:47,926
Left always means the patient's own left,

201
00:08:48,096 --> 00:08:50,115
and when you're facing someone,

202
00:08:50,165 --> 00:08:52,530
their left is on your right.

203
00:08:56,030 --> 00:08:58,445
Directional terms always compare one spot

204
00:08:58,495 --> 00:09:01,086
to another, and they come in pairs of opposites.

205
00:09:01,203 --> 00:09:03,426
Superior means toward the head,

206
00:09:03,483 --> 00:09:05,615
and inferior means toward the feet,

207
00:09:05,690 --> 00:09:08,181
so your nose is superior to your mouth,

208
00:09:08,218 --> 00:09:10,822
and your knees are inferior to your hips.

209
00:09:11,030 --> 00:09:13,237
Anterior means toward the front,

210
00:09:13,275 --> 00:09:15,275
and posterior means toward the back.

211
00:09:15,381 --> 00:09:18,294
Your breastbone is anterior to your heart,

212
00:09:18,332 --> 00:09:20,558
and your backbone is posterior to it.

213
00:09:20,721 --> 00:09:22,181
Quick check.

214
00:09:22,247 --> 00:09:24,633
Our cyclist's lying supine on the stretcher

215
00:09:24,690 --> 00:09:27,124
now, so his nose is pointing at the ceiling.

216
00:09:27,275 --> 00:09:30,143
Is his nose still anterior to his ears?

217
00:09:35,750 --> 00:09:36,788
Yes.

218
00:09:36,892 --> 00:09:39,166
Supine only tells you how he's lying.

219
00:09:39,279 --> 00:09:41,203
You still describe the body as if it were

220
00:09:41,270 --> 00:09:43,467
standing in the anatomical position,

221
00:09:43,543 --> 00:09:45,656
so the nose stays anterior.

222
00:09:49,156 --> 00:09:51,194
Picture a line running straight down the

223
00:09:51,237 --> 00:09:53,911
middle of the body, splitting it into right

224
00:09:53,967 --> 00:09:55,345
and left halves.

225
00:09:55,435 --> 00:09:57,081
That's the midline.

226
00:09:57,213 --> 00:09:59,722
Medial means closer to the midline,

227
00:09:59,760 --> 00:10:01,835
and lateral means farther from it,

228
00:10:01,873 --> 00:10:03,043
out toward the side.

229
00:10:03,244 --> 00:10:06,136
So your nose is medial to your eyes,

230
00:10:06,156 --> 00:10:08,175
and your ears are lateral to them.

231
00:10:08,307 --> 00:10:10,741
And this is where those forward palms pay

232
00:10:10,816 --> 00:10:11,400
off.

233
00:10:11,420 --> 00:10:14,062
In the anatomical position the thumb points

234
00:10:14,183 --> 00:10:17,571
away from the body, so the thumb is LATERAL,

235
00:10:17,618 --> 00:10:19,571
and the little finger is medial.

236
00:10:20,321 --> 00:10:22,137
Lots of students get this backwards,

237
00:10:22,175 --> 00:10:23,949
because they picture their hands hanging

238
00:10:24,024 --> 00:10:25,118
palms in.

239
00:10:25,257 --> 00:10:27,873
So on the forearm, the thumb side is the

240
00:10:27,939 --> 00:10:29,005
lateral side.

241
00:10:29,269 --> 00:10:32,703
Proximal means closer to where a limb attaches

242
00:10:32,753 --> 00:10:35,269
to the trunk, and distal means farther from

243
00:10:35,295 --> 00:10:35,879
it.

244
00:10:35,899 --> 00:10:38,891
So your elbow is proximal to your wrist,

245
00:10:38,911 --> 00:10:41,232
and your toes are distal to your ankle.

246
00:10:41,433 --> 00:10:44,552
On a limb you use these instead of superior

247
00:10:44,609 --> 00:10:46,816
and inferior, because they don't care how

248
00:10:46,854 --> 00:10:48,024
the limb is posed.

249
00:10:48,137 --> 00:10:50,741
Raise your arm overhead, and your hand is

250
00:10:50,795 --> 00:10:52,702
higher than your elbow in the room,

251
00:10:52,722 --> 00:10:54,590
but it's still distal to it,

252
00:10:54,628 --> 00:10:57,232
because proximal and distal follow the limb

253
00:10:57,297 --> 00:10:58,097
itself.

254
00:10:58,137 --> 00:11:00,552
So let's fix the radio call.

255
00:11:00,816 --> 00:11:03,420
A bad cut on the lateral side of the left

256
00:11:03,486 --> 00:11:05,911
forearm, just proximal to the wrist.

257
00:11:06,288 --> 00:11:08,628
Lateral pins it to the thumb side,

258
00:11:08,703 --> 00:11:10,590
left is the patient's own left,

259
00:11:10,628 --> 00:11:13,081
and proximal means toward the elbow,

260
00:11:13,109 --> 00:11:15,043
whether his arm is down or up.

261
00:11:16,057 --> 00:11:18,062
Now everyone pictures the same spot.

262
00:11:18,198 --> 00:11:19,496
Your turn.

263
00:11:19,609 --> 00:11:21,986
Say he's lying on his back and he stretches

264
00:11:22,024 --> 00:11:23,722
his arm up over his head.

265
00:11:23,923 --> 00:11:27,269
In anatomical terms, is his hand superior

266
00:11:27,319 --> 00:11:29,835
to his elbow, or is it distal to it?

267
00:11:36,462 --> 00:11:37,820
Distal.

268
00:11:37,946 --> 00:11:40,613
On a limb you use proximal and distal,

269
00:11:40,650 --> 00:11:42,650
because they follow the arm wherever it

270
00:11:42,711 --> 00:11:43,347
goes.

271
00:11:43,367 --> 00:11:45,896
And the catch is that superior would actually

272
00:11:45,971 --> 00:11:48,613
be wrong, because in the anatomical position

273
00:11:48,669 --> 00:11:50,650
the hand is below the elbow.

274
00:11:54,150 --> 00:11:57,094
Superficial means toward the body's surface,

275
00:11:57,132 --> 00:11:59,207
and deep means farther in.

276
00:11:59,333 --> 00:12:01,547
Think of the thigh in layers,

277
00:12:01,577 --> 00:12:03,132
with skin on the outside,

278
00:12:03,192 --> 00:12:06,754
then fat, then muscle, and bone in the middle.

279
00:12:06,996 --> 00:12:10,754
Skin is superficial to muscle and bone is

280
00:12:10,799 --> 00:12:13,396
deep to it, so a stab wound deep to the

281
00:12:13,449 --> 00:12:15,583
muscle has gone through the skin,

282
00:12:15,603 --> 00:12:17,245
the fat, and the muscle.

283
00:12:17,509 --> 00:12:20,943
The doctor checks how far down his cut goes.

284
00:12:21,119 --> 00:12:23,433
It's through the skin and the fat,

285
00:12:23,471 --> 00:12:25,849
but the muscle underneath hasn't been cut,

286
00:12:25,924 --> 00:12:28,339
so the wound is superficial to the muscle.

287
00:12:28,591 --> 00:12:32,037
It looked bad, but in anatomy terms it never

288
00:12:32,113 --> 00:12:33,320
got past the fat.

289
00:12:33,603 --> 00:12:36,566
The last pair is about sides.

290
00:12:36,716 --> 00:12:40,867
Ip sih lat er al means on the same side,

291
00:12:40,968 --> 00:12:44,415
so your right arm and right leg are ip sih

292
00:12:44,528 --> 00:12:48,716
lat er al, and contra lat er al means opposite

293
00:12:48,792 --> 00:12:51,735
sides, like your right arm and left leg.

294
00:12:51,916 --> 00:12:54,603
That really matters in the nervous system,

295
00:12:54,632 --> 00:12:56,716
because the main pathways for movement and

296
00:12:56,804 --> 00:12:58,603
touch cross the midline.

297
00:12:58,780 --> 00:13:01,320
So a stroke on the right side of the brain

298
00:13:01,426 --> 00:13:04,264
weakens the left arm and the left leg.

299
00:13:04,505 --> 00:13:06,301
Your turn.

300
00:13:06,528 --> 00:13:09,849
A patient's left arm and left leg are weak

301
00:13:09,912 --> 00:13:10,905
after a stroke.

302
00:13:11,119 --> 00:13:13,623
Which side of the brain was damaged?

303
00:13:19,399 --> 00:13:21,437
The right side.

304
00:13:21,588 --> 00:13:24,607
The main movement pathways cross the midline,

305
00:13:24,657 --> 00:13:27,626
so the damage is contra lat er al to the

306
00:13:27,684 --> 00:13:28,484
weakness.

307
00:13:30,324 --> 00:13:32,003
A lot of people answer left,

308
00:13:32,050 --> 00:13:34,569
because that's where the weakness is.

309
00:13:38,069 --> 00:13:39,956
So let's put it all on one picture.

310
00:13:40,157 --> 00:13:42,899
To read a term, you start at the suffix,

311
00:13:42,930 --> 00:13:44,635
then the prefix, then the root.

312
00:13:44,786 --> 00:13:47,352
For directions, the body is always standing

313
00:13:47,378 --> 00:13:49,013
in the anatomical position,

314
00:13:49,088 --> 00:13:51,277
palms forward, and right and left are the

315
00:13:51,324 --> 00:13:52,124
patient's own.

316
00:13:52,228 --> 00:13:53,390
Then the pairs.

317
00:13:53,507 --> 00:13:56,786
Superior and inferior run head to feet,

318
00:13:56,824 --> 00:13:59,428
and anterior and posterior run front to

319
00:13:59,503 --> 00:14:00,068
back.

320
00:14:00,088 --> 00:14:03,126
Medial and lateral go from the midline out

321
00:14:03,176 --> 00:14:06,069
to the side, and proximal and distal run

322
00:14:06,119 --> 00:14:06,919
along a limb.

323
00:14:06,975 --> 00:14:09,918
Superficial and deep go from the surface

324
00:14:09,994 --> 00:14:13,315
inward, and ipsi and contra mean the same

325
00:14:13,405 --> 00:14:15,126
side or the opposite side.

326
00:14:18,626 --> 00:14:20,890
As for our cyclist, he gets a few stitches

327
00:14:20,916 --> 00:14:23,153
on the lateral side of his left forearm,

328
00:14:23,173 --> 00:14:25,116
and then he's walking his bike home.

329
00:14:26,449 --> 00:14:28,588
So that's how A and P talks about the body.

330
00:14:28,720 --> 00:14:30,701
The notes and practice for this topic are

331
00:14:30,792 --> 00:14:32,815
free on levl prep dot com.

332
00:14:32,940 --> 00:14:35,456
In the next video we'll slice the body into

333
00:14:35,542 --> 00:14:38,249
planes and sections, and open up the body

334
00:14:38,343 --> 00:14:40,475
cavities where the organs sit.

335
00:14:40,579 --> 00:14:41,494
See you there.

